blind_proposals.content:
Proposition 40 is going to fail. The late polling is the strongest evidence: PPIC’s October 28 release had opposition at 52 and support at 46 among likely voters, and the Berkeley IGS poll a day later found a similar result. That is not one shaky survey; it is two independent pollsters close to Election Day showing the “yes” side behind. The campaign-finance picture points the same way: opposition committees had roughly $101 million to the support side’s $32 million as of mid-September, and total spending has topped $200 million. The opposition coalition is broad—Governor Newsom, both gubernatorial candidates, the California Teachers Association, Planned Parenthood affiliates, and several health groups—while support is concentrated in SEIU United Healthcare Workers West and Bernie Sanders. California voters are willing to tax high earners—Proposition 3 is polling at 58%—but this is a novel wealth tax with a less popular framing and a late-turning trend.

I would put the probability of passage at about 15%, with a range of 10–20%. The main uncertainty is not the direction of the evidence; it is whether late polling is understating progressive turnout or missing a late surge, and whether the “no” lead is soft enough to collapse in the final days. If I saw a final public poll showing “yes” tied or ahead, or a large late spending surge from the support side, I would move up. If a final high-quality likely-voter survey confirmed the “no” lead, I would move down to 5–10%. On the current evidence, the calibrated call is that Proposition 40 loses.

{"probability": {"low": 10, "high": 20}}

